  "mathematical_definition_of_top20_error_indicator": "z_i = 1 if |p_i - y_i| >= Quantile_0.80(|p - y|) else 0, where y_i in {0,1} and p_i in [0,1].",
  "explanation_of_discrepancy": "Because only 20.02% of benchmark events have true label y=1, and model predictions are generally conservative (p in [0.001, 0.25]), the top 20% absolute errors are virtually 100% composed of false negatives (y=1 events, where error = 1 - p >= 0.60). Consequently, AUROC(epistemic uncertainty, top20_error) is mathematically isomorphic to AUROC(epistemic uncertainty, y=1), reflecting the directional event predictability (~0.52). In contrast, continuous Spearman rank correlation (+0.56) is driven by the 80% majority class (y=0), where error |p - 0| = p scales monotonically with epistemic uncertainty.",
